WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:05.240
It's no secret that movie stars can make millions
upon millions of dollars to play someone else

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:06.240
on screen.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.150
But for some actors, money isn't everything—particularly
when it comes to sequels.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:14.201
Find out why certain household names decided
not to follow up a successful film, despite

00:00:14.201 --> 00:00:16.560
being offered a truckload of money.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:17.560
Vin Diesel

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.990
Fast &amp; Furious movies come out at a rate that's,
well, fast and furious.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:25.590
Vin Diesel was in the first film, and all
the most recent entries, but he skipped the

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:31.140
2003 sequel 2 Fast 2 Furious, despite being
offered 25 million dollars.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.579
Diesel told Hashtag Legend:

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:36.230
"Sequels had gotten such a bad name and I
had started to think of that first Fast and

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.160
Furious as a classic, like a Rebel Without
a Cause.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.280
If we 'sequelised' it the way studios were
doing at the time, just slapping a story together,

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.030
I'd ruin the chance of that first one becoming
a classic."

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.700
Ironically, Diesel used that time to film
The Chronicles of Riddick, a sequel to the

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.429
2000 movie Pitch Black.

00:00:51.429 --> 00:00:55.170
That franchise was much closer to Diesel's
heart, so much so that he took home a relatively

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.539
meager $50,000 dollars.

00:00:57.539 --> 00:00:58.659
Will Smith

00:00:58.659 --> 00:01:02.920
With The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air, Will Smith
transitioned from music into television.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.689
And with Independence Day, the top grossing
movie of 1996, Smith transitioned from TV

00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:09.659
star to full-fledged movie star.

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:11.530
"Who's the man, huh?

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:12.930
Who's the man?"

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.630
When producers approached Smith to return
for an Independence Day sequel, Smith countered

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:21.080
their initial offer with a request for $50
million dollars for two sequels.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.149
Despite getting shot down, Smith still considered
being in the movie.

00:01:24.149 --> 00:01:26.750
In 2015, the sequel's director, Roland Emmerich,
said:

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:31.289
"He just was tired of sequels and he did another
science fiction film, which was his father-son

00:01:31.289 --> 00:01:33.270
story, so he opted out."

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:34.570
Michael Keaton

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:38.240
Primarily known as a comedic actor, Michael
Keaton was a bit of a left-field choice to

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.149
star in Tim Burton's Batman films.

00:01:40.149 --> 00:01:44.060
But both Batman and Batman Returns ended up
doing incredibly well.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.270
That's why it was so shocking when Keaton
walked away from the franchise before Batman

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:48.850
Forever went into production.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.659
He also walked away from a reported $15 million
dollars.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.020
"What was it about three that you just didn't
like?"

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.020
"Sucked."

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.080
"Yeah?

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.060
There you go!

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:00.200
That pretty much says it all."

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.400
"Yeah, it just was awful."

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.749
Jodie Foster

00:02:03.749 --> 00:02:07.229
Movies that win the Academy Award for Best
Picture don't often have sequels, but The

00:02:07.229 --> 00:02:09.590
Silence of the Lambs isn't like most movies.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:12.330
"Most serial killers keep some sort of trophies
from their victims."

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:13.880
"I didn't."

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:14.880
"No.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.750
No, you ate yours."

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:21.830
After winning an Oscar for her portrayal of
FBI agent Clarice Starling, Jodie Foster was

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:25.780
offered $20 million dollars to return for
the sequel---one of the biggest actor paychecks

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:26.780
up to that time.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:31.330
But Foster said no, deciding instead to direct
a film about a circus sideshow performer called

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:32.330
Flora Plum.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.971
The film died in production, and Julianne
Moore stepped into the role of Clarice, for

00:02:35.971 --> 00:02:38.860
which she was paid a mere $2 million dollars.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:40.190
Keanu Reeves

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:42.420
The original Speed was lightning in a bottle.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:47.070
A huge box office success, it made veteran
actor Keanu Reeves a viable action star, and

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.260
thrust Sandra Bullock onto the A-list.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.920
Then there's Speed 2: Cruise Control, which
ranks among the most poorly-reviewed, franchise-killing

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:55.250
sequels of all time.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:59.920
Bullock returned, but Reeves didn't, reportedly
leaving $11 million dollars on the table.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.200
After filming the physically taxing Chain
Reaction, Reeves' manager had a simple explanation:

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.430
"He didn't want to do two action movies back-to-back."

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:08.740
Bruce Willis

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:12.510
In the first two films in The Expendables
franchise, Bruce Willis appeared alongside

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.300
an ensemble of faded-but-beloved action stars.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.310
To return for the third film, he was offered
$3 million dollars—for just four days of

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:20.310
work.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:25.640
But when Willis asked for a salary boost to
$4 million, it turns out he was, well, expendable.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.270
Producers refused the pay increase and Harrison
Ford got the part instead.

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:33.170
The Expendables 3 co-star and co-writer Sylvester
Stallone tweeted out that Willis' behavior

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:37.480
was: "Greedy and lazy...a sure formula for
career failure."

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:38.480
Katie Holmes

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.010
Dawson's Creek actress Katie Holmes starred
as Bruce Wayne's girlfriend in Batman Begins.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:46.760
But she opted out of the next movie, The Dark
Knight, choosing instead to film the forgettable

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:48.590
heist comedy Mad Money.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.400
Holmes told Business Insider in 2016 that
she doesn't regret her choice to walk away

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.270
from a critically-acclaimed blockbuster, saying,

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:58.060
"It was a decision that I made at that time
and it was right for me at that moment."

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:01.840
According to The Wall Street Journal, Holmes
would've earned between $1 and 2 million dollars

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.880
for The Dark Knight, much more than the roughly
$250,000 she got for Mad Money.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:07.880
Jim Carrey

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:12.510
1994 was the year Jim Carrey officially became
a household name, when he starred in three

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:17.040
hit movies: Ace Ventura: Pet Detective, The
Mask, and Dumb and Dumber.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.530
Carrey starred in sequels to Ace Ventura and
Dumb and Dumber, but turned down the sequel

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:23.260
to The Mask, despite a $10 million dollar
offer.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:25.930
On turning down that much cash, Carrey told
the Los Angeles Times:

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:30.060
"I don't want people to start thinking about
me as, like, a money person, cause I'm not.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.120
To me, that's a little Monopoly game that's
being played in the other room."

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.520
"That's how wealthy people laugh…

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:38.600
OH!"

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:39.600
Hilary Duff

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.160
If you weren't a tween or the parent of a
tween in the early 2000s, then the Lizzie

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.080
McGuire television phenomenon probably escaped
you.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:49.980
Disney executive Dick Cook was so confident
that a Lizzie McGuire movie would be a hit

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:55.210
that he offered lead actress Hilary Duff a
two-movie deal for $3.5 million dollars.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:58.740
Hilary's manager, who happened to be her mother,
asked for $5 million instead.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:03.060
Cook said $4 million was his final offer and
gave the Duffs a deadline when the offer would

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.060
expire.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:08.290
A day after that deadline, Duff's mom agreed
to the $4 million, but it was too late.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:11.560
And that's why there's only one Lizzie McGuire
movie.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:12.560
Will Ferrell

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:16.400
Elf is a modern-day Christmas classic, and
to revisit Buddy the Elf would be to ruin

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:17.750
the spirit of the original.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:21.720
That's a big reason why Will Ferrell refused
to make Elf 2, resisting the lure of offers

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.050
as high as $29 million dollars.

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:25.770
Regarding his decision, he told The Guardian:

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:29.860
"I remember asking myself: could I withstand
the criticism when it's bad and they say,

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.330
'He did the sequel for the money'?

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:32.870
I decided I wouldn't be able to.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:36.440
I didn't want to wander into an area that
could erase all the good work I've done."

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:41.520
"So I'm here now, I found you, daddy."

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:42.520
Thanks for watching!

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:44.950
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:47.300
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

